  "unresolvedAiThreads": [
    {
      "threadId": "PRRT_kwDORKRjBc6cAp-N",
      "replyToCommentDatabaseId": 3851460944,
      "path": "VAA.EAPI.GraphQL/VAA.EAPI.GraphQL.Domain.Trips/Queries/HolidayQuery.cs",
      "line": 500,
      "url": "https://github.com/VAAD1ST/eapi/pull/1742#discussion_r3851460944",
      "author": "copilot-pull-request-reviewer",
      "body": "`HolidayDocumentTokenHelper.ValidateAsync` can throw `SecurityTokenException` (expired/invalid token) or `ArgumentException` (missing claims). Because the GraphQL host's `ErrorFilter` treats `SecurityTokenException` as an auth-token failure, these errors will be surfaced as \"Authorization token is missing/invalid\" (401/403), which is misleading for a document-token exchange and risks leaking token-validation details when exception details are enabled. Catch token-validation exceptions here and throw a controlled `GraphQLException` with a BadRequest code/message for invalid/expired document tokens.",
      "decision": "valid",
      "resolutionComment": "Validated that HolidayDocumentTokenHelper.ValidateAsync throws SecurityTokenException derivatives for invalid or expired tokens and ArgumentException for missing required claims, while the GraphQL host maps SecurityTokenException to caller authorization failures. Updated RetrieveHolidayBookingDocument to convert those token-validation failures into a controlled GraphQL 400 response with the message 'The document token is invalid or expired.' Added a focused regression test asserting the 400 code and controlled message. Verification: 2/2 focused tests passed; TRX analysis found no failures."
    }
  ],
